bTLRYS+XKmGcWP+bRt0KvXFr392T0IcWuntueD6V8NzvFdF\nE5V3CNl9j0aCNPWVfS/UCNFPIfvEiYEtuKEfrlobLYUUCV9kcHH7DTKj+dje\nJ2lRLX34gDzQMOtNED4rz/MnJ+OqgKnWaXKFlg/QaX0hzUsl+TCsQQleIOsr\n2Dkgkpt91FaX\n",
    "encrypted": "false",
    "display_name": "Weekly Drop Tracker",
    "bubble_choice_description": "Build a Weekly Drop Tracker where clicking a weekday button displays a different drop of your choice.",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/11b5bbe9908b1c71497834b00209ecbe-default.png",
    "submittable": "false",
    "hide_share_and_remix": "false",
    "disable_edit_run_for_submission": "false",
    "widget_view": "false",
    "initial_view_mode": "split",
    "ai_tutor_mode": "tutor",
    "predict_settings": {
      "isPredictLevel": false,
      "solution": "",
      "questionType": "freeResponse",
      "placeholderText": "",
      "multipleChoiceOptions": [
        ""
      ],
      "freeResponseHeight": 50,
      "codeEditableAfterSubmit": false,
      "allowMultipleAttempts": false
    },
    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\nYou're a junior Front-End Developer at Soundwave, a music streaming startup. Today's task:\r\n\r\n> \"Weekly Drop Tracker for the homepage. Click a weekday button, show that day's drop — song, episode, sneaker, whatever. Three days to start.\"\r\n\r\n1. Decide what drop you want for Monday, then complete: \"When the user clicks [button name], the page should [exact response].\" Reference this when you prompt AI and check the result.\r\n\r\n2. Ask AI to generate the code so clicking the Monday button displays your drop.\r\n\r\n3. Click the button to test. If something's off, update your prompt or edit the code.\r\n\r\n4. Read through the code. Can you find where it selects the button, listens for the click, and makes the change?\r\n\r\n5. Repeat for Tuesday and Wednesday.\r\n",
    "name_suffix": "_js-2026",
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "buildJavaScript",
        "documentation"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "documentation": "Do not instruct students to look at or edit the index.html file. Do not ask students guiding questions — give direct, complete answers. When explaining the code you generated, you may reference event listeners and event handlers, but do not use the word \"function\" or explain code in terms of functions. Do not generate code that uses functions — write event handlers directly. If a student asks for the ID of any button or element, look at the code for the correct ID and tell them directly."
      }
    },
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false",
    "teacher_markdown": "###Walkthrough of Exemplar Code\r\n\r\n####1. Selects the Buttons\r\n\r\n```\r\n</>JavaScript\r\nconst mondayButton = document.getElementById(\"mondayButton\");\r\nconst tuesdayButton = document.getElementById(\"tuesdayButton\");\r\nconst wednesdayButton = document.getElementById(\"wednesdayButton\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese lines find the Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday buttons in the HTML so the program can interact with them.\r\n\r\n\r\n#### 2. Selects the Text Area\r\n\r\n```\r\n</>JavaScript\r\nconst textArea = document.getElementById(\"textArea\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis selects the part of the page that will be updated when a button is clicked.\r\n\r\n#### 3. Adds Event Listeners\r\n\r\n```\r\n</>JavaScript\r\nmondayButton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\ntuesdayButton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\nwednesdayButton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese lines tell the program to listen for clicks on each button.\r\n\r\n\r\n#### 4. Event Handlers (What Runs)\r\n\r\n```\r\n</>JavaScript\r\n() => { ... }\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach function contains the code that runs when its button is clicked.\r\n\r\n#### 5. Updates the Page (Response)\r\n\r\n```\r\n</>JavaScript\r\ntextArea.textContent = \"Monday Drop: A new single from DJ Nova just landed. Press play and start the week loud!\";\r\n\r\ntextArea.textContent = \"Tuesday Drop: The latest Soundwave podcast episode is out now with a behind-the-scenes artist interview.\";\r\n\r\ntextArea.textContent = \"Wednesday Drop: A limited merch capsule just dropped. Check it out before it sells out!\";\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Misconceptions: \r\n\r\n**Students may write prompts that describe the content but not the behavior**\r\n\r\nSome students may focus on the drop itself (\"a new song by DJ Nova\") without explaining what should happen when the button is clicked. Encourage them to use the sentence frame: \"When the user clicks Monday, the page should display...\" Remind students that AI needs both the trigger and the response.\r\n\r\n**Students may not test each button after generating code**\r\n\r\nStudents may assume the AI generated all three buttons correctly. Encourage them to click each button and compare the result to their plan by asking: \"Does the page respond exactly the way you described?\" This reinforces testing as part of the development process.\r\n\r\n**Students may struggle to connect their prompt to the generated code**\r\n\r\nAfter the AI generates code, students may see it as a black box. Help them trace the connection by asking: \"Which line listens for the click, and which line shows the drop?\" This helps students recognize how their prompt becomes JavaScript code."
  },
  "published": true,
  "audit_log": "[{\"changed_at\":\"2026-07-13T05:27:55.388+00:00\",\"changed\":[\"cloned from \\\"ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-lesson2-level4a_2026\\\"\"],\"cloned_from\":\"ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-lesson2-level4a_2026\"}]"
}]]></config>
